From cbc541dd266bdb7e980eec874f6c8fb82a5b41c6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 08:32:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] perf(state): keep the clean deleted-WAL holder
 verdict across unrelated directory churn

The 30 s clean verdict for the /proc (and libproc) holder walk was keyed on the
state.db directory's mtime. On hermes-hub ~/.hermes gets a new entry every ~2 s
(atomic JSON renames, lock files), so the cache never hit and every SessionDB
writer open walked every fd of every process: 3.7% of all py-spy samples of the
hub Desktop backend (producer admission sweeps, session registry, notice
delivery), t_01a1cb9d4cfc595b.

While both sidecars exist with a known birth time, the key is now their
(dev, ino, birth time) plus the directory's identity. A held retired inode is
never freed, so retiring a generation leaves the path naming nothing or another
inode. The verdict-time inodes are not held, though: once SQLite unlinks them on
the last close, ext4/xfs hand their numbers out again, so (dev, ino) alone let a
fresh pair repeat the cached key while a retired generation was still held
(review of PR #1304, real /proc repro). The birth time (Darwin st_birthtime,
Linux statx(STATX_BTIME) via ctypes) is set at allocation and untouched by WAL
writes, so it tells a reused number apart. A missing sidecar or an unknown birth
time keeps the directory mtime in the key, as before.

Inode timestamps come from a coarse clock on many kernels (one jiffy; a second
or two on some filesystems), and ext4 hands a freed number straight back, so a
same-tick unlink + recreate repeats (dev, ino, birth) and a same-tick directory
entry leaves the mtime unchanged (Blacksmith CI, second review of PR #1304). A
clean verdict is therefore only stored once every timestamp in its key is older
than 2 s; any later change then lands in a later tick and moves the key. A busy
database's sidecars are old, so its cache still hits.
